      "title": "Rescissions and dollar limits",
      "narration": "Before the ACA, some insurers canceled coverage retroactively when a member got sick, hunting the application for errors. Rescission is now permitted only for fraud or an intentional misrepresentation of a material fact, and the insurer must give 30 days’ advance notice so the member can appeal. Honest mistakes cannot cost coverage. Plans also may not impose lifetime dollar limits on essential health benefits, or annual dollar limits on them. A plan may still limit non-essential benefits and may use visit limits that are not dollar caps where permitted. The result is that catastrophic illness no longer exhausts a policy.",
